@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Frameworks}/{GlobalValues.Framework_Path_Part_Modal}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Installation}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Framework_Name_Modal @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Installation</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Framework_Name_Modal @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Installation</h1>


<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> It is a requirement that the Blazor Ramp Core <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">core-live-region.js</span> script, <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">LiveRegionService</span>, and
    associated <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component are added alongside this component’s specific
    requirements (a stylesheet reference in this instance).
        
</p>
<p>
    You do not need to add the BlazorRamp.Core package separately, as BlazorRamp.Core is included as a transitive package, you only need to add the packages that contain the components
    that you wish to use i.e the <b>BlazorRamp.DialogFramework</b> NuGet package.
</p>
<p>
    Please follow the instructions in the <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Getting_Started}{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Installation}")">@GlobalValues.Getting_Started_Name - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Installation</a>
    ensuring that in the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;head&gt;</span> section you also add the Dialog Framework stylesheet as follows:
</p>
<CodeBox CodeBlock="@ModalSnippets.Add_Dialog_Framework_Style_Sheet" AriaLabel="Add dialog frame stylesheet code block" />


<p>
    <b>Next register</b> the dialog framework service in the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Program.cs</span> file (Both server and client if using Server and WebAssembly interactive rendermode) and
    add the following line to the service registration section if you are using the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</span> package:
</p>
<CodeBox CodeBlock="@ModalSnippets.Add_Dialog_Framework_Service" AriaLabel="Register live region service code block" />
<p>
    For other Dependency Injection providers, register the type <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">ModalDialogService</span> as you normally would.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Important</strong> Please read carefully so you understand the best place for the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;ModalDialogContainer /&gt;</span> which acts as the
    root for unlimited nested dialogs for the specific process.
</p>
<p>
    <b>Adding</b> the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;ModalDialogContainer /&gt;</span>
</p>
<p>
    The best location really depends on your project type and the following is the recommendation.
    <ul>
        <li>
            <b>Interactive Server (Global or Per Page)</b> - You can simply place a single instance of the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;ModalDialogContainer @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</span> in the
            <span class="Routes.razor"></span> file next to the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Interactive WebAssembly (Global or Per Page)</b> - You can simply place a single instance of the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;ModalDialogContainer @@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" /&gt;</span> in the
            <span class="Routes.razor"></span> file next to the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component.
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <b>Interactive Auto (Server and WebAssembly) (Global or Per Page)</b>
            </p>
            <p>
                This is the most complex Blazor scenario, and as such placement is determined by your understanding of the render modes and when you want the downloading of the WebAssembly files to start.
            </p>
            <p>
                For example you can simply place two instances of the <span>ModalDialogContainer</span> in the <span class="Routes.razor"></span> file next to the
                <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component. One using <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">@@rendermode="InteractiveServer"</span> the other using
                <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">@@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly"</span> and these would handle all Server and WebAssembly page usages.<br />
                <strong>Note: You cannot add another</strong> for <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">@@rendermode="InteractiveAuto""</span>. For your Auto pages you would place an instance of the
                <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;ModalDialogContainer @@rendermode="InteractiveAuto" /&gt;</span> on each page that wants to have a dialog / nested dialogs.
            </p>
            <p>
                Placing an  <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">@@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly"</span> in this project type will cause the WASM files to download immediately as
                the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;Router &gt;</span> is run every request.
            </p>

            <p>
                If this is a little confusing then you can also just simply place an instance of the <span>ModalDialogContainer</span> on each page where you want to have a dialog / nested dialogs
                and by default it will use the parent components <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">@@rendermode</span>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Note:</strong> Unlike the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> that works without an interactive mode, the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">ModalDialogContainer</span>
                needs one to work correctly.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>
<p>
    See the next page, <b>Modal Dialog (Using)</b> for examples of how you use the dialog service to open and close dialogs, pass data in, get data out and handle the register a handler for the
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Escape</span> key. The dialog framework overrides the default HTML Dialog API so you can choose what you want to do when the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Escape</span> key
    is pressed.
</p>

